<article class="env-example" id="0H21">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H21">Example <span data-tag="0H21">100.11.9</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme and let $x \in X$ be a point. Then the monomorphism $x \to X$ is the residual gerbe of $X$ at $x$ where we, as usual, identify $x$ with the scheme $x = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\kappa (x))$. If $X$ is an algebraic space and $x \in |X|$, then the residual gerbe at $x$ (which is called the residual space) always exists, see Decent Spaces, Section <a href="/tag/06QU" data-tag="06QU">68.13</a>. </p>
</article>